5234375, -0.018798828125, -0.00316619873046875, -0.0186767578125, 0.043060302734375, 0.0186614990234375, 0.01305389404296875, 0.018402099609375, 0.00970458984375, 0.01378631591796875, 0.0179595947265625, -0.049957275390625, 0.0135345458984375, 0.007419586181640625, -0.006458282470703125, 0.044464111328125, -0.01348876953125, -0.0004949569702148438, -0.0003676414489746094, 0.0012340545654296875, -0.00676727294921875, 0.01849365234375, 0.00777435302734375, -0.005451202392578125, 0.0022144317626953125, 0.0201263427734375, -0.00714111328125, 0.016082763671875, -0.0156402587890625, 0.0240325927734375, 0.00533294677734375, 0.0221405029296875, 0.0123748779296875, -0.01430511474609375, 0.0008959770202636719, -0.005950927734375, -0.0205841064453125, -0.007476806640625, -0.0202484130859375, 0.0090789794921875, -0.0382080078125, 0.018402099609375, 0.005710601806640625, 0.0239105224609375, -0.024139404296875, -0.013671875, -0.01216888427734375, 0.0114898681640625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "How level-0 players behave and how they are perceived by higher-level players are central questions in the literature on level-k models of boundedly rational strategic reasoning. In these models, **level-0 actions** refer to the actual choices a participant makes when instructed to act as a level-0 player\u2014that is, to make a decision without any strategic consideration of others\u2019 possible moves. In contrast, **level-0 beliefs** are participants\u2019 explicit predictions about what a hypothetical level-0 player (not themselves) would do in a given game. For example, in a number-choosing game, a participant might personally choose \u201c0\u201d as a level-0 action, but believe that a generic, non-strategic player would choose \u201c5\u201d as a level-0 belief. This distinction is important because conflating the two can lead to incorrect inferences about how people reason in strategic settings and may affect the predictive accuracy of level-k models. If researchers assume that level-0 actions and beliefs are interchangeable, they risk mischaracterizing the baseline from which higher-level reasoning is modeled.\n\n**Operationalization and Elicitation Procedures**\n\nTo ensure clarity, we explicitly communicated this difference to participants during instructions and elicitation. Instructions were standardized and delivered both in written and verbal form via a computer interface in a laboratory setting. For each game, participants received the following prompts: (1) \u201cIf you were asked to play this game as a level-0 player, making a choice without considering what others might do, what would you choose?\u201d (level-0 action); and (2) \u201cWhat do you think a generic, non-strategic player\u2014someone who does not think about others\u2019 choices\u2014would choose in this game?\u201d (level-0 belief). Comprehension checks followed each prompt, requiring participants to restate the distinction in their own words before proceeding. This procedure was designed to minimize confusion and demand effects.\n\n---\n\n**Research Questions and Hypotheses**\n\nThis study investigates two main research questions: (1) Do level-0 actions and beliefs coincide in classic experimental games? (2) How are level-0 behaviors perceived by others? We hypothesize that the alignment between level-0 actions and beliefs is context-dependent and that deviations from standard level-0 assumptions can be systematically observed, depending on game features such as payoff structure and the presence of salient focal points (i.e., options that stand out due to their prominence or simplicity). The distinction between actions and beliefs is theoretically important because it affects how level-k models are specified and interpreted.\n\n---\n\n**Methods**\n\n*Participants and Recruitment*\n\nParticipants (N = 240; 54% female, mean age = 21.7 years, SD = 2.9) were recruited from a university subject pool. The sample was predominantly undergraduate students from diverse academic backgrounds. Participants were randomly assigned to teams of three to five members using a randomization algorithm to ensure no prior acquaintance within teams.\n\n*Experimental Procedure*\n\nThe experiment was conducted in a computer lab using a custom web-based interface. Each team played a series of classic experimental games, including the beauty contest and a variant of the 11-20 game. The order of games was randomized across teams to control for order effects. For each game, participants first provided individual responses to two standardized prompts (see above) regarding their own level-0 action and their belief about a generic level-0 player. Afterward, teams were instructed to discuss each game and reach consensus on both points. Teams were told to aim for unanimity, but if consensus was not reached within 10 minutes, a majority rule (simple majority vote) determined the team\u2019s final answer. All decisions were entered via the interface, which recorded both individual and team responses. No facilitators were present, but experimenters monitored for procedural compliance. Participants received a fixed show-up fee plus performance-based incentives: in each game, one team\u2019s decision was randomly selected to determine a bonus payment, with higher payoffs for correct or winning choices as specified by the game rules.\n\n*Qualitative Coding and Analysis*\n\nAll group discussions were audio-recorded and transcribed. Two independent coders reviewed the transcripts to identify recurring themes in the reasoning process. The coding scheme was developed iteratively: after an initial review of 20% of transcripts, coders met to discuss emergent themes and refine the coding manual, which included definitions, example excerpts, and decision rules for each code. The final scheme focused on references to heuristics (e.g., \u201cpick the lowest,\u201d \u201cpick the highest,\u201d \u201cchoose randomly\u201d), salience (choices that stand out due to their simplicity or extremity\u2014termed \u201csalient focal points,\u201d defined as options that are especially prominent or simple, such as zero or endpoints), and explicit rejection of randomization. Example codes included \u201crejection of randomization\u201d (e.g., \u201cNo one just picks at random; people look for something that stands out\u201d), \u201cendpoint salience\u201d (e.g., \u201cIf you don\u2019t think, you just go for the biggest number\u201d), and \u201cfocal point search\u201d (e.g., \u201cMaybe people pick the middle because it feels natural\u201d). Coders independently coded all transcripts, and inter-rater reliability was assessed using Cohen\u2019s kappa (\u03ba = 0.81), indicating substantial agreement. Discrepancies were resolved through discussion, referencing the coding manual and additional transcript examples as needed, until consensus was reached.\n\n---\n\n**Results**\n\n*Transition and Overview*\n\nWe first present quantitative results on the alignment between level-0 actions and beliefs, referencing the summary table below for clarity. We then report qualitative findings from team discussions to illustrate the diversity of reasoning strategies.\n\n*Beauty Contest Game*\n\nIn the canonical beauty contest game, where players choose numbers in an attempt to guess a fraction of the average choice, Burchardi and Penczynski (2014) found that elicited level-0 beliefs closely match observed level-0 actions, supporting the standard assumption that level-0 players choose randomly or use salient focal points (i.e., options that are especially prominent, such as zero). Our study replicates this result: in our sample (N = 60 teams), 87% of elicited level-0 beliefs matched the corresponding level-0 actions at the team level (95% CI: 78%\u201393%). Both clustered around the salient focal point of zero, with a mean choice of 0.8 (SD = 1.2) for both actions and beliefs. Over 80% of teams selected zero, with the remainder choosing small positive integers. (No figures are shown due to space constraints; summary statistics are provided in the table below.) We used a chi-squared test of independence (null hypothesis: no association between level-0 actions and beliefs) to assess whether the observed alignment exceeded what would be expected by chance (\u03c7\u00b2(1, N = 60) = 34.2, p < 0.001). Although McNemar\u2019s test is often used for paired categorical data, we used the chi-squared test here because team-level responses were categorical and not always binary, and because the primary interest was in the association between two categorical variables. The effect size (Cramer\u2019s V = 0.76) indicates a strong association, suggesting that the practical significance is substantial: when a salient focal point is present and the payoff structure is simple, participants\u2019 own non-strategic choices and their beliefs about others\u2019 non-strategic choices converge.\n\n*11-20 Game Variant*\n\nIn contrast, the pattern differs in other strategic settings. We study a variant of the 11-20 game, originally introduced by Goeree et al. (2018), in which players select integers from 11 to 20 and the highest unique number wins a prize. Our variant doubles the reward for choosing the highest unique number (from 10 to 20 points), increasing the incentive for strategic reasoning. In our experiment (N = 60 teams), only 54% of elicited level-0 beliefs matched observed level-0 actions at the team level (95% CI: 41%\u201366%), and both were widely dispersed across the available choices. The mean level-0 action was 15.2 (SD = 3.1), and the mean level-0 belief was 15.5 (SD = 3.0), with choices spread across the full range from 11 to 20. A chi-squared test of independence (null hypothesis: no association between level-0 actions and beliefs) indicated that this alignment was not significantly different from chance (\u03c7\u00b2(1, N = 60) = 2.13, p = 0.14; Cramer\u2019s V = 0.19, a small effect). The practical implication is that, in the absence of a salient focal point and with a more complex payoff structure, participants\u2019 level-0 actions and beliefs are heterogeneous and weakly aligned, challenging the generalizability of standard level-0 assumptions and reducing the predictive power of level-k models in such settings.\n\n*Summary Table: Alignment of Level-0 Actions and Beliefs*\n\n| Game                | % Alignment (Actions = Beliefs, team level) | 95% CI      | Effect Size (Cramer\u2019s V) | Focal Point Present? | Alignment Significance |\n|---------------------|---------------------------------------------|-------------|-------------------------|----------------------|-----------------------|\n| Beauty Contest      | 87%                                         | 78%\u201393%     | 0.76                    | Yes                  | p < 0.001             |\n| 11-20 Game Variant  | 54%                                         | 41%\u201366%     | 0.19                    | No                   | p = 0.14              |\n\n*Qualitative Analysis*\n\nTeam discussions in the 11-20 game revealed several recurring themes. Many teams debated whether a level-0 player would \u201cpick the lowest\u201d (e.g., 11), \u201cpick the highest\u201d (e.g., 20),"}}